---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} image={image} schemaBlocks={[articleSchema]}>
<header class="border-b border-neutral-200 bg-[#f7f2e8] py-10 md:py-16"><div class="container max-w-wide grid items-center gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2"><div><p class="eyebrow">Packing economics · <time datetime="2026-11-27">November 27, 2026</time></p><h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl font-extrabold leading-tight md:text-5xl">{title}</h1><p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-neutral-600">Put a measured value on seconds saved before accepting a more expensive carton.</p></div><img src={image} alt="Fictional Alder Quay carton beside a flat corrugated blank, stopwatch and tape dispenser on a packing bench" width="1536" height="1024" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" class="w-full rounded-xl" /></div></header>
<article class="container max-w-default prose-apex py-12 md:py-20">
<p>A carton that costs more to buy may cost less to pack. The purchasing question is how much more you can justify paying, using evidence from your own station. A supplier demonstration of one quickly opened box is a useful starting point, but it leaves out replenishment, product loading, closure and the packs that need a second attempt.</p>
<p>For Canadian and US business buyers comparing hand-assembled packaging, start with one decision: should the next order retain the current construction or fund a faster alternative? Keep the product, protection requirements and shipping route fixed. Then compare accepted, shipment-ready packs per paid labour hour. The method below works whether the alternative uses fewer folds, a different closure or a supplier-glued construction.</p>
<h2>Define what must be finished when the clock stops</h2>
<p>Write a short start-and-stop rule before timing either design. A useful boundary begins with packaging components in their normal replenishment locations and ends with the filled carton closed, checked and placed in the dispatch position. Include the label if the construction affects how it is applied. If picking the product is identical for both options, you can exclude it, but exclude it from both measurements.</p>
<p>Separately record opening bundles, bringing blanks to the station and clearing waste. Allocate these minutes across the trial output instead of hiding them outside the cycle. An alternative that opens quickly but needs frequent small-bundle replenishment may lose part of its apparent advantage. When two people work together, count both people's time: ten elapsed minutes with two operators means twenty labour minutes.</p>
<p>Identify the actual construction on the quotation and sample. The <a href="https://www.fefco.org/sites/default/files/FEFCO%20Code_WEB_Secured-LR.pdf">FEFCO design style library</a> separates slotted boxes, folder boxes and trays, and ready-glued cases into different style groups. A style reference helps describe geometry; it does not establish your packing rate. Use the <a href="/blog/rsc-boxes-vs-die-cut-boxes">RSC and die-cut comparison</a> to narrow the structures, then time the production-representative versions.</p>
<h2>Run a trial that captures awkward packs as well as easy ones</h2>
<p>Give operators a practice period before collecting results. Test both options with the normal products, inserts, closure materials and workstation arrangement. Alternate the order of the trials so the second option does not always benefit from practice or suffer from fatigue. Include the people who normally pack the order, rather than asking the most experienced operator to demonstrate every run.</p>
<p>Record total labour minutes, accepted packs, rejected components, reworked packs and the reason for each interruption. Keep a note of unusual events rather than deleting slow cycles without explanation. Repeat the comparison across representative batches; a short run can help find folding problems, but it should not become an unsupported promise for an entire shift.</p>
<p>Calculate <strong>labour seconds per accepted pack = total included labour minutes × 60 ÷ accepted packs</strong>. Rework time belongs in the numerator, and rejected packs do not belong in the denominator. Keep discarded material costs separate so they are not counted twice. For fit, closure and reference-sample controls, use the <a href="/blog/corrugated-box-sample-approval-checklist">corrugated sample approval checklist</a> alongside the timing sheet.</p>
<figure><img src="/images/blog/2026-11-27/timed-packout-trial.webp" alt="Hands loading a fabric pouch into a fictional Nori Field carton during a packing trial, with finished packs and a tally sheet nearby" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<h2>Turn twelve seconds into a maximum price premium</h2>
<p>Here is an invented budgeting example, not an Apex quotation or a productivity claim. Suppose the current pack averages 42 labour seconds per accepted unit and the alternative averages 30. The buyer uses an illustrative loaded labour rate of CAD 30 per hour. Both trials include the same activities and produce packs that meet the same acceptance requirements.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Illustrative assembly cost calculation" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Illustrative comparison at 10,000 accepted packs</caption><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Current construction</th><th>Alternative construction</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Labour seconds per accepted pack</td><td>42</td><td>30</td></tr>
<tr><td>Labour cost at CAD 30/hour</td><td>CAD 0.35</td><td>CAD 0.25</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assumed landed carton cost</td><td>CAD 0.70</td><td>CAD 0.77</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carton plus measured labour</td><td>CAD 1.05</td><td>CAD 1.02</td></tr>
<tr><td>Difference over 10,000 packs</td><td colspan="2">CAD 300 in favour of the alternative, before incremental fixed costs</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The twelve-second reduction is worth <strong>12 ÷ 3,600 × CAD 30 = CAD 0.10 per pack</strong> in the labour model. With other variable costs equal, ten cents is the maximum carton premium before the variable-cost advantage disappears. The assumed seven-cent premium leaves three cents per pack.</p>
<p>If the change also requires an incremental CAD 450 setup charge, the illustrative recovery volume is <strong>CAD 450 ÷ CAD 0.03 = 15,000 accepted packs</strong>. At 10,000 packs, the CAD 300 variable advantage does not cover that charge: the alternative remains CAD 150 more expensive in this comparison. If the variable advantage is zero or negative, increasing volume does not recover the setup cost through this saving.</p>
<p>Use one currency and a consistent delivered-cost basis. Add differences in closure consumables, material waste, replenishment, storage and freight before making the final decision. If assembly improves but the alternative occupies more warehouse space or requires a different outer shipper, include those consequences. Keep the labour rate and exchange-rate assumptions visible so purchasing can rerun the calculation when they change.</p>
<h2>Decide whether the benefit is money or available capacity</h2>
<p>Across 10,000 packs, twelve seconds releases about 33.3 labour hours. That does not automatically reduce payroll by 33.3 hours. If the same team remains on the same paid shift, the benefit may be time available for other work. A cash-saving case needs a concrete mechanism, such as avoided overtime or a lower contracted packing charge, confirmed by the relevant owner.</p>
<p>Ask operations where the constraint sits. If packing is waiting on product supply or a downstream labeller, faster carton assembly may not increase dispatch output. It can still reduce handling effort, but record that benefit separately. Do not promise additional daily shipments by dividing a shift length by a single best cycle time.</p>
<p>For outsourced fulfilment, ask whether the provider will change its fee after approving the new construction. An internal time study cannot rewrite a per-order service contract. If the fee stays the same, evaluate the change using the actual charge and any other documented benefits.</p>
<h2>Release the order only after these five approvals</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Operations:</strong> confirms the trial boundary, representative operators, replenishment allowance and measured accepted output.</li>
<li><strong>Quality:</strong> accepts product fit, closure and the complete pack for its intended handling; faster assembly alone is not protection evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Purchasing:</strong> compares the same order quantities and delivered-cost basis, including tooling, setup and consumables.</li>
<li><strong>Finance or the budget owner:</strong> distinguishes recoverable cash savings from capacity and accepts the volume used for payback.</li>
<li><strong>The supplier:</strong> acknowledges the approved structure, board specification, sample revision and permitted substitutions before production.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Send the timing sheet with the quote request</h2>
<p>Give the supplier the product dimensions and weight, current pack components, trial photographs, accepted-packs count, labour record and target order quantity. Ask which construction changes could remove the difficult steps and what samples are needed to test them. Request confirmation of carton price, fixed charges, delivered quantity, shipment configuration and the proposed production and delivery plan.</p>
<p>Apex Packaging Solutions is based in London, Ontario, serving Canadian and US businesses as a supplier and project coordinator working with qualified overseas production facilities. Project specifications, quantities, sampling and delivery arrangements require confirmation. <a href="/contact">Contact Apex with your current pack and assembly measurements</a> to discuss alternatives that can be evaluated against your actual packing operation.</p>
